ting the ip address of our  redis instance  and we're getting the redis password  again in plain text  then we're going to build our  application as a container push it to a  docker registry  and deploy it to cloud run and you'll  also notice that we're using the redis  password here  vi and environment variable to cloud run  let's go ahead and quit and let's deploy  our application  so what this is going to do is this is  going to build a container push a  container to  our google container registry and then  it's going to  ask cloud run to deploy that container  with those environment  variables our application will read  those environment variables at start  time  and deploy them so we are pushing to  container registry now and then we're  waiting for  our cloud run to pull that image and  route traffic to it  and when this is done we will get a url  where we can visit  our service  great so i'm going to go ahead and open  up this url in a browser  great here it is and as you can see this  is a very basic website  that has a hit counter that's backed by  redis every time i visit the site  i increase the hit counter i can reset  the counter  but what happens if someone breaks this  site so you'll notice  the reset counter method actually  includes the couch that i can set to  and i can set this to a negative number  and the site continues to function  but what happens if i set it to  something like a fruit  well then my whole application crashes  and we're presented this very nice  helpful debugging page that's very  similar to what you would see in like  rails or django or any popular web  framework  we get a bunch of information about the  request but we also get the environment  and you'll notice in this environment  our redis host and our redis password  are available  in plain text  anyone with access to this application  you can visit this url right now in your  browser  you would have access to this redis host  and this redis password  hopefully this makes it clear why plain  text environment variables aren't a  great idea  great let's jump back over to the slides  hopefully that made it really clear that  you should not  store secrets in plain text environment  variables  not only do you open yourself up to  classifications of vulnerabilities  it's really not a best practice so that  really only leaves the other two options  encrypted environment variables or a  centralized secrets management solution  let's take a look at encrypted  environment variables  so instead of taking our plain text  secret and putting it in an environment  variable  we can instead encrypt that secret first  what that looks like is a human or a  machine  takes the plain text secret and they  give it to a key management system like  google cloud kms  google cloud kms encrypts that plain  text data and produces what we call  ciphertext or the encrypted secret  we then provide this encrypted secret as  an environment variable to our  application  this way the environment variable has an  encrypted secret  not a plain text secret when our  application boots  it communicates with google cloud kms to  decrypt the value  and then get the plain text but that  plain text only exists  in memory it doesn't exist in the  environment  given to the serverless application  let's go ahead and take a look at a demo  great our second example is going to  leverage google cloud kms  to encrypt the secret value before we  put it in an environment variable  then when our application boots it will  decrypt the value using cloud kms  and only store the decrypted secret in  plain text  in memory so if someone gains access to  the application  or the environment of the application  they would only see the ciphertext the  encrypted  secret let's take a look at what that  looks like the first thing we have to do  is we have to update our application  i've already written some helper code  that makes this easy  so we're going to leverage this kms  decrypt method that accepts the  ciphertext or the encrypted secret via  the redispass environment variable  and then we actually have to encrypt our  secret so let's go ahead and do that  we're going to run encrypt string and  this is going to encrypt the string that  we give it which in this example  is the redis password with a google  cloud kms key  i'm going to copy this to my clipboard  and then  we also have to update our deployment  script because instead of passing in the  plain text secret  we now need to pass in this cipher text  or this encrypted secret  notice that we're still passing in it as  an environment variable but we're  passing it in as the cipher text  now let's go ahead and actually deploy  the application  and again this is going to build and  push our container  and then we're going to wait for cloud  run to deploy this new revision  and then after the revision is deployed  the application should behave exactly  the same  because we only decrypt the secret at  start time and we only eat the  additional cost of decrypting that  secret one time  let's go ahead and jump back over so  you'll notice that the application is  still  erroring because we haven't fixed the  bad value in redis  but the redis password is now that  ciphertext  and because google cloud kms is guarded  by iam  identity and access management you  cannot connect to this redis host  using this redis password because you  cannot decrypt it  unless you have access to the cloud kms  key  no this is great but there are some  disadvantages to this approach which  we'll talk about in a second  great let's jump back over to the slides  so as you can see encrypted environment  variables do protect against the  immediate threat  and they're a great starting point but  there are some drawbacks  the biggest of which is once you deploy  your serverless application with an  encrypted secret  you lose centralized auditing and  visibility into how that secrets being  used  if that's a requirement for your  organization or security practices  we need to take a look at the third  option which is a centralized secrets  management solution  in this model the application requests  its secrets directly from a secrets  management system  so as a secret admin or a developer i  put my secret into the centralized  storage system  and as an application i request that  secret directly from the storage system  that centralized storage storage system  is responsible for  encryption decryption logging and  monitoring  all in one place  on google cloud our first party secrets  management solution  is secret manager we aim to be that  centralized secret store that provides  you that value of auditing and logging  and visibility  google secret manager is a new fully  managed gcp  product that provides a secure and  convenient method  for storing api keys certificates  passwords and other sensitive data  that you might need in your applications  and services  secrets rarely differ across cloud  regions  for example your twilio api key is  unlikely to change  if you move your app from us east one to  europwest 2.  for this reason secret names in google  secret manager  are project global resources  the resource uri in this example refers  to the secret name to my secret  in the project name my project even  though that secret  name is global the underlying secret  payload  the actual api key is still stored  regionally  for example in this slide you can see  that it's stored in us east 1  u.s west one and europe north one  but how does the secret data end up in  those regions  google cloud handles it all we do this  with replication policies and we have  two replication policies  for google secret manager for customers  who don't really have a preference where  their secret payloads are stored  you should choose an automatic  replication policy  with this policy there's no  configuration and google decides the  best place to put your secret  but if you're a customer that has a  requirement that your data exists only  in certain regions  you should choose a user-managed  replication policy and pick  the specific region or regions in which  you want the secret to be stored  regardless of which policy you choose  though  google cloud handles the replication of  that secret across all of the selected  regions  you don't have to run a cloud function  or kick off a nightly cron job  google is the first public cloud  provider to offer offer a fully managed  secret replication  across regions  now i have to admit i've been lying  up until this point i keep talking about  secrets but i'm actually talking about  secret  versions that's because all secrets and  secret manager are versioned by default  the secret is actually just a very thin  wrapper around a logical collection of  versions  the secret version is what actually  stores the payload  data and state in your applications  you can pin to a specific version or you  can use the latest alias to pick up the  most recent highest numbered version  note that latest is a floating alias so  if a newer version is created  latest automatically points to that new  version  seeker manager uses an api first design  so that means that anything you can do  by clicking buttons in the cloud console  or by running commands in your terminal  via gcloud  is also possible in your favorite  programming language  in fact we have samples in seven  different languages that allow you to  call and execute secret manager directly  from your serverless applications  you can do so in ruby or python  java.net or php  go and node.js  before moving on you might be a little  bit confused about  secret manager and key manager we talked  earlier and  the second example we looked at  leveraged google cloud kms  so what is the difference between google  cloud kms  and google secret manager google cloud  kms is a cryptographic  oracle system nobody including yourself  or google can extract the keys in that  system  google cloud kms provides an api that  does high-level operations like  encrypt decrypt sign and verify using  those keys secret manager on the other  hand  is a data storage system that saves and  allows you to access  secret material so while they both  involve encryption  and decryption secret manager is more of  a storage solution whereas cloud kms is  more of a crypto oracle  let's take a look at the different user  journeys to help you understand the  difference between these two products  with google cloud kms in order to  persist a secret we have to create an  encryption key  encrypt the secret with that key and  then store the resulting ciphertext  somewhere a file system a database or  in-memory  as we saw in the second example we had  to store it in an environment variable  google cloud kms does not store the  secret that's your responsibility  and it also doesn't keep track of how  the secret is used  after it's been encrypted because google  cloud kms is concerned about the  encryption  keys not the actual data that's  encrypted or decrypted  with secret manager when you persist a  secret with secret manager you give it a  name  like twilio api key and you give it the  value that you want to store  secret manager handles the encryption  and decryption automatically  as well as the replication across  regions for availability  when you want that secret back with  google cloud kms  you have to provide google code kms the  ciphertext  and then google cloud kms will decrypt  that ciphertext and return the plaintext  value  assuming you have the proper  authorizations to do so  to retrieve a secret with secret manager  you just access the secret directly by  name  example twilio api key if you're  authorized to access the secret  secret manager will return the secret  and its metadata  let's go ahead and take a look at using  google secret manager in action  our third approach is to use google  cloud secret manager  unlike google cloud kms google secret  manager actually  stores the secret material and you only  need a reference to retrieve it  let's take a look at what this looks  like again just like before we need to  update our application  so instead of pulling the secret from  kms  we're instead going to pull the secret  from  secret manager using this access secret  function  and like i said before this code is all  open source on github and there'll be a  link in the slides and in the show notes  so you can always take a look later  let's go ahead and save this  and now we need to actually create the  secret i've written a little script  called create secret that just automates  this for us  you'll notice it uses the g-cloud  command to create the secret the secret  is named my secret  and it has a value of super secret and  then we're granting impermissions on the  secret to our cloud run service account  because secrets are denied by default so  we have to grant access  let's go ahead and run this command to  go ahead and create the secret  and it looks like it failed because i  already created it let me  fix that real fast  this is why you never do live demos  something's always bound to break  so i've just deleted that in another tab  over here  so let's try that again we'll go ahead  and create the secret  and great it succeeded you'll also  notice that  it's creating version one that's because  secret manager secrets are automatically  versioned but we're just using the  latest here  as one example and then lastly we need  to update our  deployment script so instead of having  this big long  password from cloud kms we instead just  pass in the reference to our secret  which is my secret we'll save this  and deploy the application this will  need to rebuild the container  because we change the code it'll push it  to the container registry  and then it will deploy it to cloud run  and again it will use the exact same url  just creating a new revision  so we're just waiting for traffic to  route at this point  great and it's done and 